  input tokens are 90% of the volume and cost less per token,
  output tokens are 10% of the volume and cost more

  (placeholder rates — this book prints no price list. Set RATE_* in .env
   from your provider's page and every figure below becomes yours.)

Three things in that breakdown decide every optimisation in this chapter.

The input is mostly the same text, over and over. Each model call re-sends the
system prompt, the tool schemas, and every message so far — which is why a
conversation gets more expensive per turn, and why §28.2's provider-side
prompt caching exists.

Most of the calls are not the ones you think about. An agent's visible work is
one question and one answer; the trace shows a planner, an embedding, and a
call per step. The step you never think about is often the one to remove.

And the cheapest token is the one you do not generate. Output is a minority of
the volume at several times the price, which makes §28.2 — asking for shorter
answers — the most overlooked lever in the field.
